<section xml:id="sec-8-5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Linear Programming</title>

<assemblage component="instructor">
<p>In this Lesson we consider optimization problems in two variables. We'll write and solve a linear programming problem using three steps: write the objective function and the set of constraints, graph the set of feasible solutions, evaluate the objective function at the vertices of the set of feasible solutions to find the optimum value.</p>

<p>GOALS</p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>Write an objective function and a set of constraints</p></li>     
<li><p>Find the set of feasible solutions</p></li>
<li><p>Find the optimum solution</p></li>
<li><p>Interpret the result</p></li>
</ul></p></assemblage>

<activity xml:id="act-8-5-1"><title>Maxiizing Revenue</title>

<introduction><p>Tanya makes cereamic mugs and bowls for sale at local craft fairs.</p>
<p><ul>
    <li><p>Each mug takes her 2 hours to make and 1 hour to paint.</p></li>
    <li><p>Each bowl takes 1 hour to make and 2 hours to paint.</p></li>
    <li><p>Before the next fair, Tanya has the use of a potting table for 100 hours, and she can spend 80 hours painting her creations.</p></li> 
    <li><p>She has room on her display table for at most 30 bowls.</p></li>
</ul></p>
<p>Tanya plans to sell the mugs for $15 each, and the bowls for $12 each. How many mugs and how many bowls should Tanya make in order to maximize her potential revenue?</p></introduction>

<p><ol marker="a.">
    <li><p>What are the variables in this problem?</p>
      <p><m>x = </m></p>
      <p><m>y = </m></p></li>

<li><p>Write an inequality about Tanya's use of the potting table.</p>

    <p>(1)</p>
    
